__d(function(a,e,t,s,n,i,r){n.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Social Science \u2014 Geography",textbook:"Our Environment \u2014 Textbook for Class VI",chapter_number:16,chapter_title:"Maps",estimated_study_time_minutes:45,difficulty:"Easy",learning_objectives:["Define a map and distinguish it from a globe and a sketch","Identify and explain the three components of a map: distance, direction, symbols (the 3Ds)","Explain the meaning and use of a map scale","Describe the cardinal and intermediate directions and use a compass","Explain conventional symbols and the importance of a map key/legend","Classify maps by types: physical, political, thematic","Describe a sketch map and its uses","Connect map-reading skills to Karnataka's geography and ISRO's remote sensing"],prerequisite_knowledge:["Globe as a model of Earth (Chapter 13)","Latitude and longitude as coordinates (Chapter 14)","Cardinal directions: North, South, East, West","Basic understanding of scale from mathematics"],introduction:{why_important:"Maps are among the most powerful tools ever created by humans. They compress the entire world \u2014 or any part of it \u2014 onto a manageable surface and allow us to understand spatial relationships, plan routes, manage resources, and govern territories. Every decision involving geography \u2014 from building a road to responding to a flood \u2014 uses maps.",real_life_relevance:"You use maps every day \u2014 Google Maps on your phone, route maps in buses, weather maps on TV. Every map you use applies the same principles: scale, direction, and symbols. Understanding these makes you a more capable and independent navigator and critical thinker.",karnataka_context:"Karnataka's Survey Department (Survey, Settlements and Land Records department) maintains detailed maps of all of Karnataka's 236 taluks and their land parcels. ISRO's National Remote Sensing Centre (NRSC) in Hyderabad (under ISRO's umbrella) creates satellite-based maps of India \u2014 tracking forests, rivers, crops, and urban growth. ISRO's Bhuvan geoportal (bhuvana.nrsc.gov.in) is India's version of Google Earth, providing detailed satellite imagery of all of India including Karnataka. The Survey of India (SOI) produces topographic maps covering the entire country. Bengaluru's BDA (Bengaluru Development Authority) and BBMP use GIS (Geographic Information Systems) \u2014 digital map layers \u2014 to plan roads, utilities, and land use. Western Ghats biodiversity maps, created partly by IISc Bengaluru, are used for conservation planning.",connection_to_previous_learning:"Chapters 13\u201315 established Earth's shape, coordinate system, and motions. Chapter 16 focuses on how we represent Earth (or parts of it) on a flat surface \u2014 maps \u2014 and how to read them correctly using scale, direction, and symbols."},story_summary:{characters:[{name:"Map",role:"A flat, scaled-down representation of Earth's surface (or a part of it) on a flat surface \u2014 paper, screen, or fabric"},{name:"Scale",role:"The ratio between distances on the map and actual distances on Earth \u2014 the 'translation key' from map to reality"},{name:"Direction",role:"The orientation of the map; usually shown by a North arrow; cardinal directions: N, S, E, W"},{name:"Symbols",role:"Conventional signs representing real-world features on a map \u2014 communicated through a legend/key"},{name:"Legend / Key",role:"The map's dictionary \u2014 explains what each symbol and colour means"}],plot:["The chapter opens by contrasting a globe (three-dimensional, accurate but impractical) with a map (flat, portable, practical but with some distortion for large areas).","Maps are introduced as flat representations of Earth's surface or a part of it, drawn to scale.","Three essential components of a map are introduced: (1) Scale, (2) Direction, (3) Symbols \u2014 the three pillars of map-reading.","Scale: the ratio between map distance and actual distance on the ground. Types: statement scale, representative fraction (RF), linear/graphic scale.","Direction: maps need a reference \u2014 usually North \u2014 indicated by a North arrow (compass rose). Cardinal directions (N, S, E, W) and intermediate (NE, NW, SE, SW) are covered.","Symbols: conventional signs represent features (mountains, rivers, roads, buildings) using agreed symbols so any map reader worldwide can understand them.","The map legend/key explains all symbols used on that specific map.","Types of maps: physical (terrain, elevation), political (countries, states, cities), thematic (specific topics \u2014 rainfall, population, soil types).","Sketch maps: rough, informal maps made without scale; useful for showing directions and relative positions.","The chapter closes with the importance of maps in daily life, governance, and planning."],moral:"A map is a window into space and a tool of power \u2014 whoever can read and make maps can navigate, plan, and understand the world at a glance."},core_concepts:[{concept_name:"What is a Map and How It Differs from a Globe",simple_explanation:"A map is a flat, scaled representation of Earth's surface or part of it. Unlike a globe, a map can represent any size of area and is easy to use, but it distorts shape and size for large areas.",detailed_explanation:"A map is a flat (two-dimensional) representation of Earth's surface or any part of it, drawn to a particular scale. Maps can show the entire world, a country, a state, a city, or even a single building. Advantages over globe: (1) Portable and practical \u2014 can be printed and folded. (2) Can show a specific small area in much greater detail than a globe. (3) Can show any type of information (roads, rainfall, population) as a theme. (4) Can be stored digitally. Disadvantages compared to globe: For large areas, flat maps distort shape, size, and/or distance \u2014 this is the map projection problem. For example, on a Mercator map, Greenland looks as large as Africa, though Africa is 14 times larger. A globe preserves all properties but only for the whole Earth. For small areas (like a city), maps are very accurate. The difference between a map and a sketch: A sketch is a rough, informal drawing (like a hand-drawn direction guide) made without accurate scale \u2014 useful for communication but not for precise measurement.",real_life_example:"Your phone's Google Maps is a digital map \u2014 it shows you streets, buildings, parks, and your exact location (GPS-determined latitude/longitude). It uses scale, direction (north arrow or compass), and symbols (icons for restaurants, hospitals, etc.) \u2014 all the map components you learn in this chapter. A school atlas has both a globe section and many flat maps \u2014 showing how maps serve different purposes.",karnataka_example:"ISRO's Bhuvan geoportal creates detailed satellite-based maps of Karnataka \u2014 showing land use, forest cover, river systems, and urban growth. The BBMP uses GIS (Geographic Information System) maps with multiple layers to plan Bengaluru's roads, water pipes, and drainage. Karnataka's village and urban maps are maintained by the Survey, Settlements and Land Records department under the state government.",common_mistakes:"Students think maps are always more accurate than globes. For SMALL areas (city, district), maps are very accurate. For LARGE areas (continents, world), globes are more accurate because flat maps must distort the spherical surface to make it flat. Also, students think sketch maps are a type of map with scale \u2014 they are NOT. Sketch maps are drawn without accurate scale.",memory_tip:"Map = Flat = Practical. Globe = Round = Accurate. Trade-off: flat maps distort big areas but are portable and detailed for small areas. Globe is 100% accurate but only for the whole Earth and only for what fits on a basketball-sized model."},{concept_name:"Scale \u2014 The Map's Translator",simple_explanation:"Scale is the ratio between distances on a map and the actual distances on Earth. It tells you how much the real world has been shrunk to fit on the map.",detailed_explanation:"Scale is the relationship between a distance measured on a map and the corresponding actual distance on Earth's surface. It is the most critical component of a map \u2014 without scale, you cannot use the map to measure real distances. Types of Scale: (1) Statement Scale (verbal scale): Written in words. Example: '1 cm = 10 km' or '1 centimetre represents 10 kilometres'. Easy to understand but changes when the map is enlarged or reduced. (2) Representative Fraction (RF): Written as a ratio or fraction. Example: 1:1,000,000 or 1/1,000,000. Means 1 unit on the map = 1,000,000 of the same units on the ground. Has no units \u2014 works for any unit (cm, inch, etc.). Does NOT change when the map is resized. (3) Linear/Graphic Scale: A drawn line divided into segments showing actual distances. Example: a line on the map divided into sections labelled 0, 10, 20, 30 km. Has the advantage that it remains accurate even when the map is photocopied at a different size. Large scale vs. Small scale: Large scale map = small area shown in great detail (e.g. 1:10,000 \u2014 city map). Small scale map = large area shown with less detail (e.g. 1:10,000,000 \u2014 world map).",real_life_example:"A map of Bengaluru at scale 1:50,000 means 1 cm on the map = 50,000 cm = 500 m on the ground. So if two places are 4 cm apart on this map, they are 4 \xd7 500 m = 2 km apart in reality. You use this calculation every time you plan a route using a printed map.",karnataka_example:"Survey of India (SOI) topographic maps of Karnataka are produced at 1:50,000 scale (1 cm = 500 m) for detailed planning and 1:250,000 scale (1 cm = 2.5 km) for regional overview. Karnataka's agricultural planning department uses 1:50,000 maps for watershed management and irrigation planning. ISRO's Bhuvan portal allows users to set their own viewing scale \u2014 from the entire country (small scale) down to individual buildings in Bengaluru (large scale).",common_mistakes:"Students confuse 'large scale' and 'small scale'. Large scale = LARGE detail, SMALL area (city map). Small scale = SMALL detail, LARGE area (world map). The ratio is inverse \u2014 a 1:10,000 map is LARGER scale than a 1:1,000,000 map because 1/10,000 is a bigger fraction than 1/1,000,000.",memory_tip:"Large scale = Large detail = Large on the map = Small area. Small scale = Small detail = Small on the map = Large area. Think: zoom in on your phone map = large scale. Zoom out to see the world = small scale. Scale and zoom are inversely related."},{concept_name:"Direction on a Map",simple_explanation:"Direction on a map is shown using a North arrow or compass rose. Four cardinal directions (N, S, E, W) and four intermediate directions (NE, NW, SE, SW) are used to describe positions and movements.",detailed_explanation:"Maps need a reference direction \u2014 usually North \u2014 to orient the reader. This is shown by a North arrow (an arrow pointing north) or a compass rose (a star-like diagram showing all principal directions). Cardinal directions: North (N), South (S), East (E), West (W) \u2014 the four primary directions. Intermediate directions: Northeast (NE), Southeast (SE), Southwest (SW), Northwest (NW) \u2014 midway between the cardinal directions. By convention, most maps are oriented with North at the top. In the absence of a North arrow, assume North is at the top of the map. A compass is the instrument used to find direction \u2014 its magnetised needle always points north (magnetic north), allowing the user to orient both themselves and their map correctly. To read direction on a map: face your map with North pointing to magnetic north (using a compass or landmark) \u2014 then left is West, right is East, and behind is South.",real_life_example:"When using Google Maps on your phone, you see a compass rose or North arrow in the corner. The blue dot shows your location, and the direction you are facing is shown by the phone's compass. If you are walking south, south is shown as the direction ahead on the screen (if in navigation mode). Understanding cardinal directions allows you to interpret this intuitively.",karnataka_example:"Karnataka's geography uses cardinal directions naturally: the Western Ghats run roughly north-south along Karnataka's western boundary. Karnataka's coast faces west (to the Arabian Sea). Rivers like the Cauvery flow generally east (from Kodagu) before turning south. North Karnataka (Ballari, Kalaburagi) is the direction of the Deccan Plateau. KSRTC bus route maps and BMTC Bengaluru bus maps use standard N-S-E-W orientation to help passengers navigate.",common_mistakes:"Students say the compass always points to the North Pole. A compass needle points to Magnetic North (which is close to but not exactly the Geographic North Pole). The difference is called magnetic declination \u2014 not significant for basic map reading but important for precise navigation. Also, students forget the intermediate directions \u2014 NE, SE, SW, NW \u2014 which are essential for precise direction description.",memory_tip:"Never Eat Shredded Wheat = North East South West (clockwise from top). Intermediate: NE between N and E, SE between S and E, SW between S and W, NW between N and W. Or draw a compass rose \u2014 it's a cross (+) with diagonals (\xd7) = 8 directions. Draw it and label it from memory."},{concept_name:"Symbols, Legend, and Types of Maps",simple_explanation:"Maps use conventional symbols to represent real features (mountains, rivers, roads). The legend/key explains these symbols. Maps are classified as physical, political, or thematic based on what they show.",detailed_explanation:"Symbols: Maps cannot show every real-world feature in detail \u2014 they use agreed-upon symbols (conventional signs) to represent features. Examples: blue lines = rivers, brown contour lines = elevation, dotted lines = state boundaries, red lines = national highways, green shading = forests, black dots = villages. Symbols are internationally agreed so any map-reader can understand them. A map key/legend is the box on the map that explains what each symbol represents \u2014 it is the 'dictionary' of the map. Without a legend, a map may be unreadable. Types of Maps: (1) Physical maps: Show natural features \u2014 mountains, rivers, plains, elevation (height above sea level). Use colours \u2014 blue for water, brown/green for land. (2) Political maps: Show human boundaries \u2014 countries, states, districts, capitals, cities, roads. Show administrative divisions. (3) Thematic maps: Show a specific theme or topic \u2014 rainfall distribution, soil types, population density, mineral resources, crop patterns, temperature, etc. Also called special-purpose maps. (4) Sketch maps: Informal, rough maps without accurate scale. Used for giving directions or showing relative positions.",real_life_example:"Your school atlas has: Political maps of India (showing state boundaries, capitals \u2014 political), Physical maps of India (showing Himalayas, Deccan Plateau, river systems \u2014 physical), and Thematic maps (rainfall distribution, soil types, mineral resources \u2014 thematic). Each uses different colours and symbols explained in its own legend.",karnataka_example:"Karnataka has specific thematic maps: Rainfall map (showing Western Ghats with 3000+ mm, north Karnataka with 500mm). Soil map (red laterite soils in coastal region, black cotton soils in north Karnataka). Mineral map (iron ore in Ballari, manganese in Shimoga). Crop map (paddy in coastal, ragi in south Karnataka, cotton in north). These are all thematic maps used by government departments for planning. ISRO's NRSC creates land-use maps, vegetation maps, and disaster risk maps of Karnataka using satellite imagery.",common_mistakes:"Students think symbols on maps are always pictures of the thing they represent (e.g. a picture of a tree for forests). In reality, symbols are often abstract or stylized \u2014 a green patch for forest, a black square for a building, a blue line for a river. The legend explains each one. Also, students confuse political and physical maps \u2014 political = human-made boundaries; physical = natural terrain features.",memory_tip:"Three types of maps = PPT: Physical (mountains, rivers), Political (borders, cities), Thematic (special topic). Legend = map's dictionary. No legend = can't read the map. Conventional symbols = the shared language of maps \u2014 the same blue line means 'river' on maps worldwide."}],key_vocabulary:[{term:"Map",definition:"A flat, scaled-down representation of Earth's surface or a part of it"},{term:"Scale",definition:"The ratio between distances on a map and the corresponding actual distances on Earth's surface"},{term:"Statement scale",definition:"Scale expressed in words: e.g. '1 cm = 10 km'"},{term:"Representative Fraction (RF)",definition:"Scale expressed as a ratio: e.g. 1:1,000,000 \u2014 1 unit on map = 1,000,000 units on ground"},{term:"Linear/Graphic scale",definition:"Scale shown as a drawn line divided into segments representing actual distances"},{term:"Large scale map",definition:"Shows a small area in great detail (e.g. city map at 1:10,000)"},{term:"Small scale map",definition:"Shows a large area with less detail (e.g. world map at 1:10,000,000)"},{term:"Cardinal directions",definition:"The four primary compass directions: North (N), South (S), East (E), West (W)"},{term:"Intermediate directions",definition:"The four directions midway between cardinal directions: NE, SE, SW, NW"},{term:"Compass",definition:"An instrument with a magnetised needle that always points north, used to find directions"},{term:"Symbols (conventional signs)",definition:"Agreed-upon marks used on maps to represent real-world features"},{term:"Legend / Key",definition:"The box on a map that explains the meaning of all symbols and colours used"},{term:"Physical map",definition:"A map showing natural features \u2014 mountains, rivers, plateaus, plains, and elevation"},{term:"Political map",definition:"A map showing human-made features \u2014 national and state boundaries, cities, capitals, roads"},{term:"Thematic map",definition:"A map showing a specific theme or topic \u2014 rainfall, population, soil types, minerals, crops"},{term:"Sketch map",definition:"A rough, informal map drawn without accurate scale, used for showing directions or relative positions"}],quick_check_questions:[{after_concept:"Scale",questions:[{type:"Recall",q:"Name three types of scale used on maps.",a:"Statement scale (e.g. 1 cm = 10 km), Representative Fraction (e.g. 1:1,000,000), and Linear/Graphic scale (a drawn line divided into distance segments)."},{type:"Application",q:"On a map with scale 1 cm = 5 km, two cities are 6 cm apart on the map. What is the actual distance?",a:"6 cm \xd7 5 km/cm = 30 km actual distance."}]},{after_concept:"Symbols, Legend, and Types of Maps",questions:[{type:"Recall",q:"What is a legend (key) on a map?",a:"A legend is a box on the map that explains the meaning of all symbols and colours used \u2014 it is the 'dictionary' of the map."},{type:"Understanding",q:"Differentiate between a physical map and a political map.",a:"Physical map: shows natural features \u2014 mountains, rivers, plains, elevation. Political map: shows human-made features \u2014 countries, states, cities, boundaries."}]}],key_takeaways:["A map is a flat, scaled representation of Earth's surface \u2014 practical and portable, unlike a globe.","Three essential components of a map: Scale, Direction, Symbols (the 3Ds \u2014 distance, direction, design/symbols).","Scale types: Statement scale (words), Representative Fraction (ratio), Linear/Graphic scale (drawn line).","Large scale = small area, large detail. Small scale = large area, small detail.","Cardinal directions: N, S, E, W. Intermediate: NE, NW, SE, SW. Compass points north.","Maps are oriented with North at the top by convention.","Symbols are conventional signs on maps; the legend explains them.","Types of maps: Physical (natural features), Political (boundaries and cities), Thematic (special topics like rainfall, soil).","Sketch maps are informal maps without accurate scale.","ISRO's Bhuvan portal and GIS systems create digital maps of Karnataka for planning and research."],mind_map:{root:"Maps",branches:[{branch:"Map vs. Globe",sub:["Globe = 3D, accurate","Map = flat, practical","Map distorts large areas but shows detail for small areas"]},{branch:"Scale",sub:["Statement: 1 cm = 10 km","RF: 1:1,000,000","Linear/Graphic scale","Large scale = small area; Small scale = large area"]},{branch:"Direction",sub:["North arrow/compass rose","Cardinal: N, S, E, W","Intermediate: NE, NW, SE, SW","Compass = magnetised needle \u2192 North"]},{branch:"Symbols & Legend",sub:["Conventional signs = agreed symbols","Legend = map's dictionary","Blue = water, brown contours = elevation"]},{branch:"Types of Maps",sub:["Physical: mountains, rivers","Political: borders, cities","Thematic: rainfall, soil, crops","Sketch: rough, no scale"]},{branch:"Karnataka Links",sub:["ISRO Bhuvan portal \u2014 satellite maps","SOI topographic maps","BBMP GIS maps for Bengaluru","State Survey Dept \u2014 land maps"]}]},revision_notes:"Chapter 16 (Geography) covers maps. Map = flat, scaled representation of Earth's surface. Globe = 3D and more accurate for large areas; map = flat, portable, practical. Three map components: (1) Scale \u2014 ratio of map distance to real distance. Types: Statement (words), RF (ratio), Linear (drawn line). Large scale = small area, great detail. Small scale = large area, less detail. (2) Direction \u2014 North arrow or compass rose. Cardinal: N/S/E/W. Intermediate: NE/SE/SW/NW. Compass points north. (3) Symbols \u2014 conventional signs explained in legend/key. Types of maps: Physical (natural features), Political (borders, cities), Thematic (specific topics). Sketch = rough map, no scale. Karnataka: ISRO Bhuvan portal, SOI maps, BBMP GIS, state Survey dept.",must_remember_facts:["Map = flat 2D representation of Earth's surface, drawn to scale.","Three map components: Scale, Direction, Symbols.","Scale types: Statement (1 cm = X km), RF (1:N), Linear (drawn bar).","Large scale = small area + great detail. Small scale = large area + less detail.","Cardinal directions: N, S, E, W. Intermediate: NE, NW, SE, SW.","Compass needle always points north.","Map legend/key = explains all symbols used on the map.","Types: Physical (terrain), Political (borders), Thematic (special topic), Sketch (rough, no scale).","Representative Fraction (RF) 1:1,000,000 means 1 unit on map = 1,000,000 units on ground.","ISRO's Bhuvan geoportal = India's satellite image map platform, created using remote sensing."]}},2556,[]);